# 6. Key Design Decisions (IMPORTANT)

## ❗ aiogram + FastAPI together

* aiogram runs inside same Python app
* no separate bot service for MVP

---

## ❗ No queue system

* AI calls are synchronous (acceptable for MVP)

---

## ❗ No real-time processing

* everything is post-session only

---

## ❗ Telegram voice chat is external

* we do NOT build RTC
* we only orchestrate rooms via links

---

## ❗ Room moderation vs user flow

* **User flow** (invite from DB → join group → return to bot) is unchanged; **`invite_link`** remains stored only in PostgreSQL.
* **Admin flow** uses the same bot with **allowlisted** operator ids (e.g. **`ADMIN_TELEGRAM_IDS`**) to act on a room’s **`telegram_chat_id`**.
* **Telegram Bot API limits:** there is **no** method to list all members of a large group; “remove everyone” applies to **known** user ids (tracking strategy is an implementation detail). There is **no** one-shot “delete all messages forever” for the whole chat; deletion uses per-message or batch APIs within Telegram’s rules.

---

## ❗ Schema and tests

* **Alembic** is the only supported way to evolve the database schema.
* New **`services/`** and **`repositories/`** code follows **§8** testing expectations.

---

# 7. MVP Success Definition

MVP is successful if user can:

1. Join bot
2. Enter room (via DB-backed invite link)
3. Talk in group
4. Send voice message to the bot
5. Confirm they finished
6. Receive AI feedback

👉 If this loop works end-to-end — MVP is valid

